================================================================================================================
V2.  ALGEBRAIC IDENTITY: DOES THE KERNEL SURVIVE THE PARTIALLING AT ALL?
================================================================================================================
  On the isolated branch g_pred = sqrt(g_N a0) = sqrt(G M a0)/R and g_obs = 3 sigma^2/R, so R CANCELS:
      residual = log10(3 sigma^2) - 0.5 log10(G M a0)  =  2 log sigma - 0.5 log M* + const(a0).
      check: r(residual_isolated, 2logS - 0.5logM) = +1.000000 (must be exactly 1)
      check: sd of (residual_isolated - alg) = 2.48e-16 (must be 0 -- pure constant)
      partial(residual_isolated, beta | logM) = +0.6911
      partial(log sigma        , beta | logM) = +0.6911   <-- identical by the identity above
      partial(residual_MAXBRANCH, beta | logM) = +0.7172  (the published number)
  [FAIL] V2 (WHAT THE CLAIMED NUMBER ACTUALLY MEASURES) if the partial correlation is evidence about the FRAMEWORK, it must change when the framework's acceleration constant or kernel changes.  On the isolated branch a0 enters the residual as a pure ADDITIVE CONSTANT, so the partial is identically the correlation between anisotropy and line-of-sight dispersion at fixed stellar mass -- a statistic with no framework in it at all   (partial(residual_isolated, beta|logM) = +0.691073 is EXACTLY partial(log sigma, beta|logM) = +0.691073, to machine precision, because residual = 2logS - 0.5logM + const. a0 CANCELS. The published +0.717 is the same statistic with 2 of 8 objects moved onto the EFE branch. So 'the deficit correlates with beta at fixed mass' is, arithmetically, 'sigma correlates with beta at fixed M*'.)

================================================================================================================
V3.  IS +0.717 > +0.667 A STRENGTHENING?  DEGREES OF FREEDOM
================================================================================================================
  raw     r = +0.6666  t = 2.191 on 6 df  ->  p = 0.0710   (null sd 0.378, z = 1.764)
  partial r = +0.7172  t = 2.301 on 5 df  ->  p = 0.0697   (null sd 0.408, z = 1.757)
  g03's own permutation p's: raw 0.0820 vs partial 0.0774 -- a shift of 0.005, i.e. NOTHING.
  covariance carried by beta BEFORE partialling (in r units) +0.6666; AFTER +0.2465 -- the shared signal SHRANK by 63 per cent; the coefficient only rises because the DENOMINATOR (residual variance left after mass) shrank faster.
  fraction of residual variance already explained by logM alone = 0.851
  [FAIL] V3 (THE WORD 'STRENGTHENS') for the claim to mean what it says, the partial must be more significant than the raw correlation once the lost degree of freedom is accounted for   (p goes 0.0710 -> 0.0697 (t-test) and 0.0820 -> 0.0774 (g03's own permutation). In standardised units z = 1.764 -> 1.757. THE CORRELATION DOES NOT STRENGTHEN; it is unchanged to three decimal places in significance. The rise from 0.667 to 0.717 in the COEFFICIENT is the arithmetic consequence of dividing by sqrt((1-rxz^2)(1-ryz^2)) = 0.3437, i.e. of removing 85 per cent of the residual's variance, not of any gain in evidence.)

================================================================================================================
V4.  THE PERMUTATION NULL IS THE WRONG NULL
================================================================================================================
  naive shuffle-beta permutation  p = 0.0777   [g03 reports 0.0774 -- reproduced]
  Freedman-Lane permutation       p = 0.0777   (preserves r(beta,logM) = -0.455)
  [PASS] V4 the p-value attached to the claim must come from the null the claim is about ('beta carries nothing beyond mass'), not from the stronger null that beta is unrelated to mass as well   (naive 0.0777 vs Freedman-Lane 0.0777. The gap is 0.0000. The published p uses the naive shuffle. Whether or not the gap is large, the quoted p answers a different question than the claim asks.)

