================================================================================================================
V7.  THE DECIDER -- SPEARMAN HEADLINE vs PEARSON PARTIAL
================================================================================================================
  Every headline number in g03 is a SPEARMAN rank correlation: checks A2, A3, A4, A7, the substitution
  table A9, the Monte Carlo A12 and the VERDICT all quote rho = +0.667.  The confound test A5 alone
  switches to a PEARSON partial.  Pearson and Spearman happen to coincide at +0.667 for the RAW
  correlation here, which is why the swap is invisible in the text.  They do NOT coincide after
  partialling.  Doing the partial in RANKS -- the estimator the rest of the file uses -- is the
  apples-to-apples comparison.

      RAW  Pearson  r   = +0.6666      RAW  Spearman rho = +0.6667   (they coincide)
      PEARSON  partial|logM = +0.7172  perm p = 0.0750  (1.78 sigma)  <- the claim
      SPEARMAN partial|logM = +0.1721  perm p = 0.6976  (0.39 sigma)  <- rank-consistent
      KENDALL  partial|logM = +0.2728   (third estimator, agrees with Spearman)

  WHY THEY DISAGREE.  partial = (rxy - rxz*ryz)/sqrt((1-rxz^2)(1-ryz^2)).  rxz (residual vs logM) is
  -0.92 either way.  The direction is set entirely by ryz = corr(beta, logM):
      Pearson  r(beta, logM) = -0.4554     Spearman rho(beta, logM) = -0.6667
  The Pearson value is ATTENUATED, and by exactly one object.  Fornax sits at log M* = 7.63 with the
  next object at 6.74 -- a leverage of h_ii = 0.69 out of 1 in the log M* regression:
        Draco       logM = 5.46  z = -0.87  leverage h_ii = 0.221
        Sculptor    logM = 6.36  z = +0.37  leverage h_ii = 0.142
        Fornax      logM = 7.63  z = +2.13  leverage h_ii = 0.693
        Carina      logM = 5.58  z = -0.71  leverage h_ii = 0.188
        Sextans     logM = 5.64  z = -0.62  leverage h_ii = 0.174
        Leo I       logM = 6.74  z = +0.89  leverage h_ii = 0.225
        Leo II      logM = 5.87  z = -0.31  leverage h_ii = 0.137
        Ursa Minor  logM = 5.46  z = -0.87  leverage h_ii = 0.221
      drop Fornax: Pearson r(beta,logM) goes -0.455 -> -0.715, and the Pearson partial FALLS to +0.611

  leave-one-out, both estimators:
        drop Draco      : Pearson partial = +0.694   Spearman partial = +0.008
        drop Sculptor   : Pearson partial = +0.765   Spearman partial = +0.073
        drop Fornax     : Pearson partial = +0.611   Spearman partial = +0.550
        drop Carina     : Pearson partial = +0.896   Spearman partial = +0.444
        drop Sextans    : Pearson partial = +0.775   Spearman partial = +0.471
        drop Leo I      : Pearson partial = +0.657   Spearman partial = +0.200
        drop Leo II     : Pearson partial = +0.618   Spearman partial = -0.079
        drop Ursa Minor : Pearson partial = +0.597   Spearman partial = +0.008
  [FAIL] V7 (THE DECIDER) the claim 'partialling out stellar mass STRENGTHENS the correlation' must hold under the estimator the rest of the file uses for every other number.  g03's headline is Spearman; its confound test is Pearson   (SPEARMAN: rho +0.667 -> partial +0.172, permutation p 0.698 = 0.39 sigma. KENDALL: +0.571 -> +0.273. Both rank estimators say partialling out stellar mass DESTROYS the correlation (74 per cent of it gone, p = 0.70). Only the Pearson version rises, and it rises because Pearson understates r(beta,logM) as -0.455 against the rank value -0.667, an attenuation caused by Fornax alone (leverage 0.69).)

  Local Volume Database footing: raw r = +0.6112, partial|logM = +0.5816  [g03 says +0.582]

================================================================================================================
VERDICT
================================================================================================================
  The ARITHMETIC of the claim is CORRECT and reproduces exactly.  What is wrong is the INFERENCE drawn
  from it.  (i) 0.667 -> 0.717 is not a strengthening: p goes 0.071 -> 0.070 (t) and 0.0820 -> 0.0774
  (the file's own permutation).  The coefficient rises only because partialling removes 85 per cent of the
  residual's variance and the partial-correlation denominator divides it back out.  (ii) On the isolated
  branch the residual is ALGEBRAICALLY 2 log sigma - 0.5 log M* + const(a0), so the partial is exactly
  corr(beta, log sigma | log M*) -- a0 and the kernel cancel, and the statistic contains no framework.
  (iii) The bootstrap interval on the partial spans -0.101 to +0.988 and includes zero.
  (iv) DECISIVE: the file's headline statistic is SPEARMAN everywhere except this one check. The rank-
  consistent partial is +0.172 with permutation p = 0.698 (0.39 sigma), and Kendall's gives +0.273.
  Partialling out stellar mass DESTROYS the correlation under both rank estimators. The Pearson rise is
  driven by Pearson understating r(beta,logM) (-0.455 vs rank -0.667), an attenuation
  produced by Fornax alone (log M* = 7.63, leverage 0.69). THE DIRECTION OF THE CLAIM IS AN ESTIMATOR CHOICE.

